  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nFlash flooding concerns continue today with rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms moving across the forecast area ahead of an \napproaching cold front that will cross this afternoon. Ongoing and \nnew flash flooding is expected from showers and thunderstorms \nthrough the afternoon and especially this evening from the crossing \ncold front.\n\nBetween 3\" and 6\" of rainfall has fallen across portions of \nsoutheastern Ohio and western West Virginia this morning leading to \nsignificant flash flooding. A conveyor belt of light showers will \ncontinue to move up from the southwest this morning. Models show \nanother heavier round of showers and thunderstorms that will form \nlater this afternoon and evening. This will be in the 5-10 PM time\nframe today. Models still vary as to where the heaviest rain\nwill occur. The HRRR brings the heaviest rainfall across the\nTri-State and I-64 Corridor through the Metro Valley. The NAM on\nthe other hand, chooses to keep it north further impacting\nlocations that saw significant flooding this morning. An\nadditional 1-3\" of rainfall is possible through tonight.\n\nA conditional severe weather threat exists this afternoon and \nevening for damaging wind gusts with any strong to severe \nthunderstorms. Cloud cover may ultimately keep a widespread severe \nweather threat at bay, but forcing from the crossing front this \nafternoon will possibly create some stronger isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms.\n\nLight showers and drizzle will continue into Friday morning as\nthe front exits east. High pressure will sneak in late on Friday\nproviding a brief drier spell ahead of our active weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA warm front will lift north on Saturday bringing a widespread\nsoaking rain back to the area. The highest amounts around 1\" to\n1.50\" looks to remain across the mountains and down into southwest\nVirginia. Localized flash flooding could be possible into the \nweekend with rounds of showers and thunderstorms impacting the \narea. \n\nHigh pressure is projected to move in on Monday and the\nconsensus is drier weather that will span Monday through\nTuesday.",
